8. SEAMLESS SPACE AND PLANET TRANSITIONS
==================================================

THIS IS ONE OF THE MOST IMPORTANT GAMEPLAY REQUIREMENTS.

The player must be able to pilot the spacecraft continuously.

I DO NOT WANT THE PLAYER TO PRESS E TO ENTER OR EXIT A PLANET.

I DO NOT WANT PLANETARY TRAVEL TO BE A TELEPORTATION MECHANIC.

I DO NOT WANT ORDINARY PLANETARY TRAVEL TO USE A LOADING SCREEN.

The spacecraft must be physically controlled by the player.

### TAKEOFF

When flying away from the surface:
- Activate spacecraft engines.
- Retract landing gear.
- Show engine exhaust.
- Increase altitude naturally.
- Stream planetary terrain appropriately.
- Gradually blend atmospheric fog.
- Transition sky color.
- Decrease atmospheric scattering.
- Reveal stars.
- Reveal the planet curvature.
- Enter open space naturally.

### PLANET APPROACH

When flying toward another planet:
- Detect proximity.
- Begin atmospheric entry.
- Increase atmospheric effects.
- Blend cloud layers.
- Show heating effects where appropriate.
- Progressively reveal terrain.
- Stream terrain chunks.
- Reveal vegetation and environmental details.
- Display weather conditions.
- Identify suitable landing locations.

### AUTOMATIC LANDING

When the ship enters a valid landing condition:
- Reduce speed.
- Find an appropriate landing surface.
- Align the ship.
- Lower landing gear.
- Complete landing automatically.

Do not require an E-key interaction.

The player should remain in control of the flight experience.

### PLANETARY TRAVEL

Allow the player to fly manually between nearby planets.

Create a destination navigation system.

Allow optional assisted navigation.

Warp travel must be integrated with spacecraft controls.

Warp effects must include:
- Space distortion.
- Star streaks.
- Engine effects.
- Camera effects.
- Audio transition.
- Destination arrival.
- Smooth return to normal space.

Transitions must not suddenly teleport the spacecraft between two unrelated scenes during ordinary flight.

Use asynchronous generation and streaming.

Keep planetary identity consistent between distant representation and high-detail terrain.

Use appropriate floating-origin or local coordinate systems to avoid precision issues.

